Commerce Secretary, Shri Rajesh Agrawal, held high-level meetings in Brussels with Ms. Ditte Juul Jørgensen, Director-General, Directorate-General for Trade and Economic Security (DG TRADE); Mr. Koen Doens, Director-General, Directorate-General for International Partnerships (DG INTPA); and Mr. Gerassimos Thomas, Director-General, Directorate-General for Taxation and Customs Union (DG TAXUD), European Commission. The meetings focused on strengthening the comprehensive economic and strategic partnership between India and the European Union. During the meeting with Ms. Ditte Juul Jørgensen, both sides discussed measures to facilitate two-way trade, improve market access, strengthen supply chain resilience and create new opportunities for businesses in India and the European Union. They reaffirmed their commitment to a fair, balanced and mutually beneficial agreement and agreed to maintain close engagement to address the remaining procedural and implementation-related issues. In the meeting with Mr. Koen Doens, both sides exchanged views on strengthening India–EU development cooperation and strategic partnerships, with discussions covering sustainable development, clean energy, green technologies and related areas. The meeting with Mr. Gerassimos Thomas focused on customs cooperation, trade facilitation and preparations for the implementation of the India–EU FTA. Both sides recognised the importance of efficient, transparent and digitally enabled customs procedures in supporting businesses, reducing transaction costs and ensuring the smooth movement of legitimate trade. Shri Agrawal also met stakeholders from the diamond industry, including representatives associated with the Antwerp diamond ecosystem. The discussions covered the global diamond value chain, certification and traceability requirements, responsible sourcing, and opportunities arising from India's growing diamond market and manufacturing ecosystem. India and Belgium have identified gems and jewellery as an important area for deeper cooperation, with Antwerp, Mumbai and Surat recognised as major diamond centres. The Commerce Secretary participated in a BusinessEurope roundtable with European industry representatives to discuss opportunities for strengthening trade and investment ties, improving market access, enhancing supply chain resilience and advancing cooperation under the India–EU FTA. He highlighted India's strong economic growth, reform momentum and investment opportunities across key sectors, and invited European businesses to deepen their engagement with India and participate in its expanding value chains. The engagements reflect the growing breadth of India–EU cooperation across trade, investment, development partnership, taxation and customs, and reaffirm the shared commitment of both sides to further strengthening their strategic partnership. Commerce Secretary Shri @RajeshAgrawal94, Commerce Secretary, had an informal interaction with representatives of the diamond industry at the Embassy of India Brussels on 30 Oct 2026. 

He exchanged views with industry members on global diamond trade, emerging market trends, and highlighted the long-standing India-Belgium linkages in the diamond value chain.
@PIB_India — Dept of Commerce, GoI (@DoC_GoI) November 1, 2026 Commerce Secretary Shri @RajeshAgrawal94, and Ambassador Shri Pranay Verma addressed a business roundtable co-hosted by the Embassy of India in Brussels and BusinessEurope on 30 October 2026, highlighting the India-EU FTA and its potential to boost trade, investment and business partnerships between India and the EU.

